WEBVTT

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:04.964
A browser agent downloads bills from a supplier's portal.

00:00:05.264 --> 00:00:09.733
Every night it logs in, walks through the pages, and saves each invoice.

00:00:10.383 --> 00:00:13.410
One night, the portal changed its next page button.

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:16.960
It was still on the screen, but it didn't load anything.

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:19.316
Here's the next morning's trace.

00:00:19.616 --> 00:00:21.947
Step four hundred twelve, click next page.

00:00:22.247 --> 00:00:22.835
Okay.

00:00:23.135 --> 00:00:24.328
Read the page.

00:00:24.628 --> 00:00:28.391
Invoices one to twenty, of three hundred forty.

00:00:28.691 --> 00:00:29.431
Click again.

00:00:29.731 --> 00:00:30.318
Okay.

00:00:30.618 --> 00:00:31.595
Read again.

00:00:31.895 --> 00:00:33.655
Invoices one to twenty.

00:00:34.305 --> 00:00:37.656
All the way to step two thousand three hundred eighteen.

00:00:38.306 --> 00:00:40.871
Nobody wrote a bug into that loop.

00:00:41.171 --> 00:00:44.801
It simply had no end, other than when the model thinks it's done.

00:00:45.101 --> 00:00:46.915
And the model never thought so.

00:00:47.215 --> 00:00:52.013
In this lesson, you'll learn to spot this smell before the bill does, and what a

00:00:52.013 --> 00:00:53.212
real end looks like.

00:00:54.412 --> 00:00:56.548
Look at two details in that trace.

00:00:57.198 --> 00:01:00.932
The click answers okay, even when nothing changes.

00:01:01.582 --> 00:01:05.802
And reading the page always shows the same first twenty invoices.

00:01:06.102 --> 00:01:09.417
The evidence that the agent was stuck was right there.

00:01:09.717 --> 00:01:11.519
But no code was looking at it.

00:01:12.169 --> 00:01:13.753
Here's the shape of the smell.

00:01:14.053 --> 00:01:17.318
The model requests an action that looks sensible.

00:01:17.968 --> 00:01:22.316
The tool responds without an error, even with no real progress.

00:01:22.966 --> 00:01:27.110
Every turn resends the whole history, which grows with each step.

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:31.362
The loop's only exit is the model deciding to stop.

00:01:32.012 --> 00:01:36.929
And when it doesn't decide, the provider's invoice is what stops the run.

00:01:37.579 --> 00:01:38.708
How do you spot it?

00:01:39.008 --> 00:01:44.317
The agent's code says while true, or while not done, with no step counter.

00:01:44.967 --> 00:01:48.022
There's no time, token or money limit per run.

00:01:48.672 --> 00:01:53.332
Nobody can say what happens if the model never gives a final answer.

00:01:53.632 --> 00:01:58.188
And the month's most expensive run cost dozens of times the median.

00:01:58.838 --> 00:02:03.769
The trace shows the same tool, with the same arguments, several times in a row.

00:02:04.419 --> 00:02:07.628
And the only alarm that goes off is the credit card's.

00:02:08.828 --> 00:02:10.615
The prototype was born this way.

00:02:10.915 --> 00:02:15.557
The simplest agent examples use while true, and the loop shipped unchanged.

00:02:16.207 --> 00:02:17.977
It works in testing.

00:02:18.277 --> 00:02:22.041
On demo tasks, the model finishes in five steps.

00:02:22.341 --> 00:02:25.524
Nobody tested the portal with a broken button.

00:02:26.174 --> 00:02:28.208
Tools that lie by omission.

00:02:28.508 --> 00:02:33.125
Okay, as the result of a click, doesn't say whether anything changed.

00:02:33.425 --> 00:02:36.600
The model has no way to notice it's stuck.

00:02:37.250 --> 00:02:39.206
And the provider looks like a cap.

00:02:39.506 --> 00:02:43.567
People assume the context window will eventually stop the agent.

00:02:43.867 --> 00:02:47.274
But many frameworks trim old history to make it fit.

00:02:47.574 --> 00:02:49.397
And then the loop runs forever.

00:02:50.597 --> 00:02:53.506
Now the cost, with illustrative numbers.

00:02:53.806 --> 00:02:58.495
Say the system prompt and the tool descriptions are three thousand tokens, and each

00:02:58.495 --> 00:03:00.133
turn adds about four hundred.

00:03:00.433 --> 00:03:05.309
A normal run of ten turns sends about fifty-two thousand tokens in total.

00:03:05.609 --> 00:03:10.511
A stuck run of two hundred turns sends about eight point six million.

00:03:11.161 --> 00:03:16.665
Twenty times more turns, and over one hundred sixty times more tokens.

00:03:16.965 --> 00:03:21.732
Cost grows with the square of the step count, because every turn pays again for

00:03:21.732 --> 00:03:22.999
everything before it.

00:03:23.299 --> 00:03:27.653
A single stuck run can cost more than a whole day of normal use.

00:03:28.303 --> 00:03:30.110
And money is the smaller part.

00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:35.761
The loop eats the account's request quota, and takes down other runs sharing the

00:03:35.761 --> 00:03:36.364
same key.

00:03:37.014 --> 00:03:42.878
If the stuck tool has a side effect, like sending, creating or paying, every turn

00:03:42.878 --> 00:03:43.674
repeats it.

00:03:44.324 --> 00:03:48.388
And a trace with thousands of steps is close to unreadable.

00:03:49.588 --> 00:03:50.940
Here's one for you.

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:53.448
An agent runs in while true.

00:03:53.748 --> 00:03:58.692
But its tool has a rate limit of ten calls per minute, so it never spends much per

00:03:58.692 --> 00:03:59.114
minute.

00:03:59.414 --> 00:04:01.428
Is this an unbounded loop?

00:04:04.478 --> 00:04:05.048
Yes.

00:04:05.348 --> 00:04:08.425
A rate limit controls speed, not the total.

00:04:08.725 --> 00:04:13.528
A loop stuck at ten calls a minute still makes over fourteen thousand calls a day.

00:04:13.828 --> 00:04:16.185
It only spreads the cost over time.

00:04:16.485 --> 00:04:19.772
And the context window isn't a reliable brake either.

00:04:20.072 --> 00:04:24.876
Many frameworks drop old messages to fit, and the loop keeps paying for a full

00:04:24.876 --> 00:04:26.231
window, on every turn.

00:04:27.431 --> 00:04:31.061
The treatment is a refactoring called add loop budget.

00:04:31.361 --> 00:04:33.558
And the budget has four parts.

00:04:33.858 --> 00:04:36.144
The last one is the one people forget.

00:04:36.794 --> 00:04:37.992
A step cap.

00:04:38.292 --> 00:04:39.684
Pick it from your traces.

00:04:39.984 --> 00:04:44.979
If ninety-nine percent of runs finish within fifteen steps, a cap of forty leaves

00:04:44.979 --> 00:04:47.261
room, without allowing a whole night.

00:04:47.911 --> 00:04:49.735
Time and cost caps.

00:04:50.035 --> 00:04:52.345
Steps can be cheap or expensive.

00:04:52.645 --> 00:04:58.149
A money limit per run catches the case where a few steps send huge contexts.

00:04:58.799 --> 00:05:00.309
A repetition detector.

00:05:00.609 --> 00:05:06.269
The same tool, with the same arguments, three times in a row, means the agent is

00:05:06.269 --> 00:05:06.693
stuck.

00:05:06.993 --> 00:05:08.315
Stop before the cap.

00:05:08.965 --> 00:05:10.899
And behavior at the cap.

00:05:11.199 --> 00:05:15.769
Return what was done, explain where it stopped, and record the case.

00:05:16.069 --> 00:05:20.960
An agent that stops with a silent exception, and makes the task disappear, still

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:22.855
leaves the user with no answer.

00:05:23.155 --> 00:05:26.267
The cap exists, but the treatment isn't finished.

00:05:26.917 --> 00:05:32.009
Most agent frameworks already give you the first piece, as a maximum number of

00:05:32.009 --> 00:05:33.837
turns, or a recursion limit.

00:05:34.137 --> 00:05:35.336
You write the rest.

00:05:35.636 --> 00:05:38.832
And along with the budget, review your tools.

00:05:39.132 --> 00:05:44.975
A result that says what changed, like page two of seventeen loaded, helps the model

00:05:44.975 --> 00:05:46.595
notice when it's stuck.

00:05:46.895 --> 00:05:51.519
The pattern you're heading for is called stop conditions, and it's two lessons

00:05:51.519 --> 00:05:51.875
ahead.

00:05:52.525 --> 00:05:57.317
And a week after you set the cap, say two percent of runs end there.

00:05:57.617 --> 00:05:59.824
Don't raise the cap to four hundred.

00:06:00.124 --> 00:06:05.739
Without reading the traces, you'd only let stuck runs spend ten times more before

00:06:05.739 --> 00:06:06.363
stopping.

00:06:06.663 --> 00:06:08.359
And don't ignore them either.

00:06:08.659 --> 00:06:14.468
Read those traces, find the cause, like a vague tool result or a page that changed,

00:06:14.468 --> 00:06:16.988
and turn each one into an eval case.

00:06:17.288 --> 00:06:19.982
They're the best source of hard cases you have.

00:06:21.182 --> 00:06:23.071
Two cases to sharpen the eye.

00:06:23.371 --> 00:06:28.485
A job walks three hundred invoices, calling the model once per invoice, with no step

00:06:28.485 --> 00:06:28.972
counter.

00:06:29.272 --> 00:06:30.439
Is that this smell?

00:06:30.739 --> 00:06:31.139
No.

00:06:31.439 --> 00:06:35.919
The list is finite, and the code decides the next turn, not the model.

00:06:36.219 --> 00:06:39.192
The loop ends at invoice three hundred, either way.

00:06:39.842 --> 00:06:45.011
And an agent with a thirty step limit, where each step can spawn subagents, which

00:06:45.011 --> 00:06:48.393
also have thirty steps, and can also spawn subagents.

00:06:48.693 --> 00:06:50.049
Each level has a cap.

00:06:50.349 --> 00:06:52.179
The total doesn't.

00:06:52.479 --> 00:06:55.201
That's a related smell, subagent recursion.

00:06:55.501 --> 00:06:58.977
The budget has to cover the whole tree, not each node.

00:07:00.177 --> 00:07:01.521
When can you relax?

00:07:01.821 --> 00:07:07.310
A coding assistant in your terminal, with you watching and able to interrupt at any

00:07:07.310 --> 00:07:09.095
time, tolerates a high cap.

00:07:09.395 --> 00:07:10.444
Set one anyway.

00:07:10.744 --> 00:07:15.799
And no production agent, running without a person watching, should ever run without

00:07:15.799 --> 00:07:16.164
a cap.

00:07:17.364 --> 00:07:19.620
So here's the idea to take with you.

00:07:19.920 --> 00:07:23.691
The model can say it's done, but it can't be the only brake.

00:07:23.991 --> 00:07:29.566
Steps, time and money belong to your code, along with a repetition check and a clear

00:07:29.566 --> 00:07:31.358
answer when the cap is hit.

00:07:31.658 --> 00:07:35.295
Then the most expensive run of the month is a number you chose.

00:07:35.945 --> 00:07:40.679
In the next lesson, we'll build that budget step by step: add loop budget.

00:07:40.979 --> 00:07:41.733
See you there.
